# Kavach — template. Copy to .env and fill in. This file IS committed,
# so it must never contain a real key.
#
# WHERE THIS ACTUALLY TAKES EFFECT
#
# These are read by the serverless function in api/analyze.ts, which runs on
# Vercel — not by the browser bundle. They deliberately have no VITE_ prefix,
# because anything prefixed VITE_ is compiled into the client bundle and would
# be readable by anyone who opens devtools.
#
#   Production  -> set them in Vercel: Settings > Environment Variables
#                  (or: vercel env add OPENROUTER_API_KEY)
#   Local       -> this file, and run `vercel dev` rather than `npm run dev`.
#                  Plain `vite dev` and `vite preview` do NOT run the /api
#                  function, so the cloud engine will simply be unavailable and
#                  the rules engine answers alone. That is a working state, not
#                  a broken one.
#
# The Vercel CLI is not installed in this project. To use `vercel dev`:
#   npm i -g vercel && vercel link

# Required for the cloud engine. Get one at https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Optional. Defaults to google/gemini-2.0-flash-001 (see api/analyze.ts).
# Any OpenRouter model id that supports JSON output will work.
KAVACH_CLOUD_MODEL=
